## Origin of opioid equivalence and eqipotency

Opioid medications are an important pillar of analgesia in medicine, used across a range of specialities and patients. There are a wide range of opioids, some naturally derived with others synthetic, each with their own potency. Whilst a wide range of factors may influence the potency of a specific opioid in a specific patient, there has long been a need to calculate the eqi-analgesic dose, or the dose of a medication that would provide an equal amount of analgesia compared to a reference. 

The need for agreed conversion values has lead to a number of publications over the last 20 years aiming to formalise the differing potencies. Much of this literature comes from the domain of palliative care in the context of opioid rotation and looked at multiple single opioid small studies to establish conversion values. McPherson described a range of limitations in the use of these equialangesic tables including the source of the data, lack of patient specific variables and bi-directionality of equivalence values. @mcphersonWhyEquianalgesicTables2020. Despite this, a set of approximate conversion values, taken with clinical experience offer a helpful set of starting points when prescribing different opioids 

The equi-analgesic conversion set have offered a new opportunity in research, the ability to quantify the total opioid burden for a group of patients.  With the increase in electronic health records, opportunities to now use total opioid use as either a feature or an outcome has become increasingly prevalent. In the last 5 years there have been 4,031 papers using the terms *"Milligram morphine equivalent"* (MME) or *"Oral morphine equivalent"* (OME) (see @sec-appendix-pubmed-1). 

In 2016 Neilsen et al published on the first formal OME conversion tables for the intention of research @nielsenSynthesisOralMorphine2016. The authors aimed to source a range of national guidelines and recommended a single value for each opioid and route with a upper and lower quartile. This "common conversion set" paved the way for the idea that all opioid research should be using a single unifed conversion set. It was clear in the literature that using a range of different conversion sets would not produce equitable or comparable results. To the extent that in 2022 the CDC produced their own value set an obligated any NIH researches working in opioid research to use their opioid conversions @dowellCDCClinicalPractice2022. 

A range of papers have highlightes some of the limitations and issues with opioid conversions sets in research. Namely the lack of accounting for inter-patient variability and the differential applicaiton of conversion sets. \[Add citations\]

Two aspects that have not been covered are the dependance on the proportion of each opioid and the error within the conversion values used. 
\[Add citations about who said/didnt say what\]


## Calculating opioid equivalence in clinical practise

Often in clinical practice the term potency is used to describe the relative strength of one opioid compared to another. The eqi-potency of a medication is commonly understood to be the dose required to achieve equal effect, and in the case of opioids, the equianalgesic effect. Thus @eq-potency describes the relationship between the conversion value $c$ and the potency $\rho$ of a given medication $i$ via a given route $r$.

$$c_{i,r} = \frac{1}{\rho_{i,r}}
$$ {#eq-potency} 

The value of $c$, then allows for the calculation of the equivalent effective dose of a target medication $d_{\text{target}}$, given a dose of a reference medication $d_{\text{referance}}$ (@eq-equivelent-dose). 

$$
d_{\text{referance}} \times c_{i,r} = d_{\text{target}}
$$ {#eq-equivelent-dose}

A full worked example of this is process of opioid conversion calculation is provided in @sec-conversion-example.^[This approach is used clinically for opioid conversion or rotation and is the origin of the opioid equivalence system. With a small set of opioid equivalence values, it's possible to convert doses between a wide range of different opioids, often via the reference medication as an intermediate.]

## Calculating opioid equivalence in research

The technique of comparing opioid equivalent doses has lead to the development of a new metric, the sum opioid burden. By converting each medication into its equi-analgesic dose of the referance medication (almost exclusivly oral morphine) and then calculating the sum, one could estimate the total opioid analgesic effect administered. 

The opioid burden metric is often presented as the weighted sum of opioids administered. We can define $\text{OME}_j$ as the sum of the dose $d$ for medication $i$ in the set of medications $\mathbf{M}$ multiplied by the conversion value $c$ for a given route $r$ for patient $j$ as shown in @eq-ome.^[Medications administered in different dose units are generally converted to a single dose unit (often **mg**) and the conversion values updated to reflect this but in some cases they may be kept in their administered unit (**e.g. mcg**) and the conversion shifted by an appropriate factor.] $\text{OME}_j$ can often be expressed as a total of all opioids administered or within a specific time frame (*e.g.* 24 hours post-operative).

$$\text{OME}_j = \sum_{i\in\mathbf{M}} d_i c_{i,r}
$${#eq-ome}

This is a powerful metric as it allows for the estimation of the total burden of pain. Assuming a patient's pain is well controlled and the analgesia provided is due predominalty to the opioid burden then the total opioid burden of a patient $\text{OME}_j $ is equivelent to the total pain burden (@eq-pain-burden). 

$$
\begin{align}
\text{Pain}_{\text{total}} &= \text{Pain}_{\text{experienced}} + \text{Analgesia} \\[1em]
& \text{Where} \qquad  \text{Pain}_{\text{experienced}} \approx 0 \\
& \text{and }  \qquad \text{Opioid burden} \approx \text{Analgesia} \\
& \text{then: }  \qquad  \text{OME}_j   \approx \text{Pain}_{\text{total}}
\end{align}
$$ {#eq-pain-burden}

The ability to now approximate the total burden of pain in a specific and quantified value offers an attractive primary outcome for any study looking to reduce the amount of pain caused. However, whilst $\text{OME}_j$ provides an estimate of a specific patients opioid burden, for use within research, further statistical tools are often applied for hypothesis testing. Given a likley parametric distribution, the arithmetic mean of a groups OME value $\mu_{\text{OME}}$ is often calculated as the sum of OME values in the group $G$ divided by the group size as shown in as @eq-mu-ome-1.
$$
\mu_{\text{OME}}^{(G)} = \frac{1}{|G|} \sum_{j=G} \text{OME}_j
$${#eq-mu-ome-1}

As the mean OME is a **mean of weighted sums** it can instead be expressed as a **weighted sum of mean doses** whereby the mean dose of a given medication within the group $\bar{d_i}$ is weighted by the conversion values $c_{i,r}$ as shown in @eq-mu-ome-final. This rearrangement is provided in @sec-equation-proof.

$$\mu_{\text{OME}}^{(G)}  = \sum_{i\in\mathbf{M}} \bar{d_{i}} c_{i,r} 
$${#eq-mu-ome-final}

@eq-mu-ome-final shows that the group mean $\mu_{\text{OME}}$ calculated for group $G$ is dependant on the relative proportions of the medications within each group. For two groups $A$ and $B$ where the mean dose of a given opioid $i$ is not then same ($\bar{d_{i}}^{(A)} \neq \bar{d_{i}}^{(B)}$) then the resulting means OME values are not equivalent ($\mu_{\text{OME}}^{(A)} \not\equiv \mu_{\text{OME}}^{(B)}$). Additionally, we see that the conversion value $c_{i,r}$ is a scaler applied to the mean dose ${\bar{d_i}}$. The accuracy of the conversion values (how close the value used for $c_{i,r}$ is to the true value) is not well described. 

## Aims

We will aim to:

1. Investigate the methods of calculating total opioid burden in the peri-operative and surgical literature.

2. Simulate and describe the dependence of differing opioid proportions and errors in conversion values on the results of an experiment. 

# Methods

## Rapid Literature Review

A rapid literature review of the use of opioid equivalence metrics in peri-operative and surgical studies over 2025 was performed. A structured search of the PUBMED database as detailed in @sec-appendix-pubmed on 7th July 2026. Papers were screened and data extracted using the Covidence platform @zotero-item-670 with a custom proforma (@sec-appendix-cov-extraction). Where conversion values were cited or written within the study, these values were extracted and compiled.








```{r}
# TO get screening data:
# Covidence -> Export -> References subsection -> Title and absract screening -> Export as .csv -> Drag to data foler -> Rename to lit_screen.csn
lit_screen <- read.csv("data/lit_screen.csv")
lit_select <- read.csv("data/lit_select.csv")
lit_included <- read.csv("data/lit_included.csv")
lit_excluded <- read.csv("data/lit_excluded.csv")
lit_data <- read.csv("data/lit_data.csv") %>% filter(Reviewer.Name == "Dylan Whitaker")
conversion <- read.csv("data/conversion_sets.csv")

n_lit_data <- nrow(lit_data)

```








## Simulated study

Two simulated studies were performed to demonstrate the sensitivity of the opioid burden metric to differences in the proportions of medications between groups and the conversion values used. The first, @sec-manuscript-simple-sim uses a simplied simulation set up to understand the variation in results across the full of the event space and the second, @sec-manuscript-exemplar-sim takes a realistic hypothetical experimental setup to establish the extent of the variation. 

### Simple simulation {#sec-manuscript-simple-sim}








```{r sim simple}

p_min <- -1
p_max <- 1
e_min <- -1
e_max <- 1
n = 10000
mu_opioid_requirment_control = 10
sigma = 0.1
treatment_effect = 0

simple_results <- data.frame(p = numeric(), e = numeric(), diff = numeric())
for(p in seq(p_min, p_max, by = 0.05)){
  for(e in seq(e_min, e_max, by = 0.05)){
    
    diff <- sim_simple(
      n = n,
      delta_p_i = p,
      sigma = sigma,
      mu_opioid_requirment_control = mu_opioid_requirment_control,
      potency_oxycodone = 2,
      error_in_c_oxycodone = e,
      treatment_effect = treatment_effect
    )
    
    simple_results <- rbind(simple_results, data.frame(p = p, e = e, diff = diff))
  }
}
```








The first simulation aimed to describe the relationship between the **difference in proportion** of a binary choice of medication between two groups, $\Delta p_i$ and the **error in the conversion value** used for the non-reference medication $\delta c_{i}$ on the observed difference in mean OME between the two groups ($\Delta \mu_{\text{OME}}$). 

In this simulation, `r comma(n)` patients were created and divided equally into two groups, control and intervention. For each patient an opioid was randomly sampled of either morphine or oxycodone to give a distribution set by $\Delta p_i$. For each patient a expected opioid requirement is sampled from a normal distribution with mean of `r mu_opioid_requirment_control` and standard deviation of `r sigma`. For patients the medication was sampled to be oxycodone, the expected opioid requirement is converted to a oxycodone dose using `potency_oxycodone` and then converted back into expected dose of morphine (the OME) using the conversion factor `potency_oxycodone` +/- an error term $\epsilon$. The difference in the total OME $\Delta \mu_{\text{OME}}$ between each group was then calculated and reported. For this simulation, the effect size between the control and treatment groups was set to `r treatment_effect`,thus $\mathbb{E}(\text{OME})_{\text{control}} = \mathbb{E}(\text{OME})_{\text{intervention}}$ and hence the expected value of $\mu_{\text{OME}} = 0$. The simulation was then repeated the range of grid values for $\Delta p_i$ and $\epsilon$ with all other parameters the same. 

$$
\begin{align}
\text{for } i \in \{\text{morphine, oxycodone} \} \\[1em]
\Delta p_i &= \Delta p_{\text{oxycodone}} = p_{\text{oxycodone}}^{\text{(control)}} - p_{\text{oxycodone}}^{\text{(intervention)}} \\
\delta c_i &= c_{\text{oxycodone}} + (c_{\text{oxycodone}} * \epsilon) \\
\Delta \mu_{\text{OME}} &= \mu_{\text{OME}}^{\text{(control)}} - \mu_{\text{OME}}^{\text{(intervention)}}
\end{align}
$$ 
### Exemplar simulation study {#sec-manuscript-exemplar-sim}








```{r}
case_volume <- 100
proportion_robotic <- 0.8
t_0 = as.Date("2020-01-01")
t_end = as.Date("2025-12-31")
t_robot = as.Date("2023-01-01")
proportion_morphine = 0.7
oxycodone_shortage = c(as.Date("2024-01-01"), as.Date("2024-12-31"))

mu_tot = 50
sd_tot = 10
potency_oxycodone_truth = 1.85
potency_oxycodone_ome = 2

annual_n = 30
robot_effect_size = 0.90
robot_effect_mg = mu_tot * (robot_effect_size-1)
```








Following this, a realistic exemplar study was performed to, the summary of the study set up is provided in @nte-sim-design. The difference in proportion between groups is simulated using a medication shortage that alters practice. Due to temporal differneces in the groups, this shortage effects one group to a greater extent thus scaling the change in proportion in a divergent manner. In this simulation the effect size of the robot is set to `r robot_effect_mg` OME mg. 

::: {#nte-sim-design .callout-note title="Simulated trial design" collapse=true}
A research team at St Somewhere Hospital want to establish if the use of a robot for a surgical procedure, reduces the post-operative opioid requirement.

They design a cohort controlled trial with two intervention groups:
  1) robot-assisted surgery - known as "robotic"
  2) standard open surgery - known as "non-robotic"
  
The primary outcome is the total opioid requirement, as measured in OME mg, in the first 24 hours post-operatively as a surrogate for the total pain caused by the surgical procedure.

The usual case volume is `r case_volume` procedures per year, these were all performed using standard surgery until `r t_robot` when a robot was acquired, and since `r scales::percent(proportion_robotic)` of cases are performed with the robot. Data was collected between `r t_0` and `r t_end`.

All patients are treated with a Patient Controlled Analgesia (PCA) after their operation and on average the anaesthetist will prescribe a morphine PCA to `r scales::percent(proportion_morphine)` of patients and an oxycodone PCA to the remainder. No other opioid medication was administered. 

During the study there was a national shortage of oxycodone from `r oxycodone_shortage[1]` to `r oxycodone_shortage[2]` and at which time all patients were prescribed a morphine PCA. Whilst the authors described this in their methods section, they did feel it would impact their results as the differing opioids were already accounted for in their OME metric. 

Patients in the standard surgery group have an expected opioid requirement ($\mu_{\text{OME}}$) of `r mu_tot`mg (sd `r sd_tot`) of morphine equivalents in the first 24 hours post-operatively. The authors expect that the robot-assisted surgery will reduce the opioid requirement by `r scales::percent(1-robot_effect_size)` and thus the expected opioid requirement for this group is `r mu_tot * (robot_effect_size)`mg of morphine equivalents in the first 24 hours post-operatively. The ground truth oxycodone potency is `r scales::percent(potency_oxycodone_truth)` relative to morphine morphine and thus the conversion value for oxycodone ($c_{\text{oxycodone}}$) is `r 1/potency_oxycodone_truth`. 
:::

All simulations were performed in `r version$version.string` and all code is available in @sec-sim. 

# Results

## Rapid Literature Review 

Of the 480 studies identified in the rapid literature search, `r nrow(lit_data)` fit the eligibility criteria and data was extracted. 

The majority of studies were cohort studies and most commonly looked at anaesthetic type interventions (@fig-study-types)








```{r}
#| label: fig-study-types
#| fig-title: Bar charts of litrature study design and intervention types
#| fig-cap: "Bar charts to summarise the types of studies. Left hand side demonstrated the count of each study type and the right hand sid demonstrates the count of each intervention type studied."
#| fig-width: 10
#| fig-height: 5

# Plot for Study.type proportions
plot_lit_study_type <- ggplot(lit_data, aes(x = Study.type)) +
  geom_bar(aes(fill = Study.type)) +
  labs(x = "Study design",
       y = "Count") +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme_academic()+
  scale_y_continuous(expand = expansion(mult = c(0, 0.05))) +
  scale_fill_academic()+
  theme(legend.position = "none")

# Plot for Intervention proportions
plot_lit_intervnetion_type <- ggplot(lit_data, aes(x = Intervention)) +
  geom_bar(aes(fill = Intervention)) +
  labs(x = "Intervention",
       y = "Count") +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme_academic()+
  scale_y_continuous(expand = expansion(mult = c(0, 0.05))) +
  scale_fill_academic()+
  theme(legend.position = "none")

plot_lit_study_type + plot_lit_intervnetion_type

```








With regards to the opioid conversion sets used `r lit_data %>% filter(Opioid.conversion.set == "Cited") %>% nrow()` cited another paper, whilst `r lit_data %>% filter(Opioid.conversion.set == "Included in text") %>% nrow()` included the conversion values in the methods. However for `r lit_data %>% filter(Opioid.conversion.set == "Not described") %>% nrow()` (`r scales::percent(lit_data %>% filter(Opioid.conversion.set == "Not described") %>% nrow()/n_lit_data)`) papers did not include any reference or citation to the opioid conversion sets used. Only `r lit_data %>% filter(Opioid.proportions.within.group == "Described") %>% nrow()` (`r scales::percent(lit_data %>% filter(Opioid.proportions.within.group == "Described") %>% nrow()/n_lit_data)`) papers described the proportions of opioids used between intervention groups as shown in @fig-study-results. 








```{r}
#| label: fig-study-results
#| fig-title: Proportion of studies reporting conversion set and opiooid proportions
#| fig-cap: "Heatmap depicting the relative size of each group by the opioid conversion value set supplie and if the opioid proportions within groups was reported. Count of observations within each group shown in text."

# Prepare data for heatmap: sum of occurrences per combination
heatmap_data <- lit_data |>
  group_by(Opioid.conversion.set, Opioid.proportions.within.group) |>
  summarise(count = n(), .groups = "drop")

# Prepare data with a conditional color column for labels
ggplot(heatmap_data, aes(x = Opioid.proportions.within.group, y = Opioid.conversion.set, fill = count)) +
  geom_tile() +
  geom_label(aes(label = paste0("n=",count),
                 color = after_scale(ifelse(fill > 0.5, "black", "white"))),
             fill = alpha("white", 0.7), size = 3.5) +
  labs(x = "Proportion within group",
       y = "Opioid conversion set",
       fill = "Count") +
  theme_minimal()+
 theme(
    axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 45, hjust = 1),
    axis.text.y = element_text(angle = 45),
    panel.grid = element_blank(),
    legend.position = "none")
```








### Conversion values

Of the `r lit_data %>% filter(Opioid.conversion.set == "Cited") %>% nrow()` papers were a conversion value set was cited, `r nrow(conversion)` unique papers were extracted. Generally across the literature, conversion values with the intention of research were in the minority (`r percent((nrow(conversion %>% filter(aim == "for research")))/(nrow(conversion)))`) and most had compiled the conversion value sets for use in clinical care. 

## Simulation study 

### Simple simulation {#sec-manuscript-results-simple}

`r comma(nrow(simple_results))` different simulations where performed across the grid values of $\Delta p_{\text{oxycodone}}$ between `r p_max` (all of the control group were treated with morphine and all of the control group were treated with oxycodone) and `r p_min` (the inverse) and $\epsilon$ between `r e_min`(estimated value was 1) and `r e_max` (estimated value was double the true value). 








```{r}
#| label: fig-simple-grid-1
#| title: "Heatmap of the observed difference group means across the grid values of differing opioid proportion and and the error in conversion value" 

library(latex2exp)

plot_simple_grid <- ggplot(simple_results, aes(y = p, x = e, fill = diff)) +
  geom_tile() +
  labs(,
       y = TeX(r'($\Delta  p_i$ (Difference in proportion))'),
       x = TeX(r'($\epsilon$ (Error in Conversion Value for Oxycodone) )'),
       fill = TeX(r'(Observed difference ($\Delta \mu_{{OME}})$)')
       ) +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(
    axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 45, hjust = 1),
    panel.grid = element_blank(),
    legend.position = "bottom")+
  scale_fill_gradientn(
  colours = c("blue", "dodgerblue", "white", "orange", "red"))+
  geom_vline(xintercept = 0, linetype = "dashed", color = "black")+
  geom_hline(yintercept = 0, linetype = "dashed", color = "black")+
  annotate("text",x=-0.7, y=0.05, label = "Proportion of medication equal")+
  annotate("text",x=-0.7, y=0.95, label = "Control group: 100% morphine\nIntervention group: 0% morphine")+
  annotate("text",x=-0.7, y=-0.95, label = "Control group: 100% oxycodone\nIntervention group: 0% oxycodone")+
  annotate("text",x=-0.05, y=-0.7, label = "No error in conversion value", angle=90)+
  annotate("text",x=0.7, y=-0.05, label = "Overestimate potency ->" )+
  annotate("text",x=-0.7, y=-0.05, label = "<- Underestimate potency")+
  scale_y_continuous(expand = expansion(mult = c(0, 0.05))) +
  scale_x_continuous(expand = expansion(mult = c(0, 0.05))) 

plot_simple_grid

```








@fig-simple-grid-1 demonstrates the observed difference between the control group and treatment group. Where the proportion of medication in the control and intervention groups are the same ($\Delta p_i = 0$) then we note that the true effect size of 0 is recovered, regardless of any error in the conversion value ($\delta c_i$) used. Also, where the conversion values used are correct to the ground truth, any difference in proportion is accounted for and the true effect is again recovered. However, as the proportions between the control and treatment group deviate, resulting difference observed is biased by any error in the conversion values. Where the groups are completely unmatched ($\Delta p_i \in 1,-1$) the resulting observed difference is the due entirely to the error in potency.

@eq-results-simple-1 demonstrates the hyperbolic parabolic relationship observed in @fig-simple-grid-1. 

$$
\Delta \mu_{\text{OME}} = -1 \cdot \Delta p_i \cdot \delta c_i
$$ {#eq-results-simple-1}

@fig-appendix-simple-sim-mu shows that the scaling constant (1 in this simulation) is the expected dose for the control group. This relationship can be rearranged as @eq-results-simple-2 (as expanded in in @eq-results-simple-2-proof).

$$
\mu^\text{intervention} = \mu^\text{control}[ 1 + \Delta p_i \cdot \delta c_i]
$$ {#eq-results-simple-2}
 
However, in all of the simulations described thus far the treatment effect was set at 0 meaning $\mathbb{E}(\text{OME}^\text{control}) = \mathbb{E}(\text{OME}^\text{intervention})$. This demonstrated that despite have no true treatment effect, difference in the proportion and error in conversion size, can lead to the observation of a treatment effect. This phenomenon can be explained by Simpsons paradox, whereby the observed treatment effect is conditioned on the proportion of the the medications within each group, thus creating the observation of a false treatment effect @pearlBookWhyNew2019. 

@sec-appendix-sim-2 demonstrates that as the treatment effect is increased (of decreased) it stretched the hyperbolic parabola in the Z direction. 

This saddle geometry illustrates a critical vulnerability in aggregate OME comparisons: if a conversion factor for the medication $i$ is over-estimated ($\epsilon_i > 0$) and prevalence is simultaneously higher in the intervention group ($\Delta p_i > 0$), the error in the effect size compounds quadratically. Conversely, opposing directional errors will collapse and at the extreme values may invert the direction of the observed treatment effect.

### Exemplar simulation study








```{r }
df <- sim_patient(
  mu_tot = mu_tot,
  potency_oxycodone_truth = potency_oxycodone_truth,
  potency_oxycodone_ome = potency_oxycodone_ome,
  proportion_morphine = proportion_morphine,
  oxycodone_shortage = oxycodone_shortage,
  t_0 = t_0,
  t_end = t_end,
  t_robot = t_robot,
  annual_n = annual_n,
  robot_effect_size = robot_effect_size
)

```








`r nrow(df)` procedures were simulated using the parameters described in @nte-sim-design. The OME value for each patient was calculated use $c_{\text{morphine}}$ as 1 (as the reference medication) and $c_{\text{oxycodone}}$ as `r 1/potency_oxycodone_ome`, in contract to the ground truth value of `r 1/potency_oxycodone_truth`. The mean dose (mg), opioid and OME value for each group is shown in @tbl-sim-demo.








```{r}
#| label: tbl-sim-demo
#| tbl-title: Demographic table of simulated study population

library(tableone)

table_1 <- CreateTableOne(data=df,
               vars = c("dose", "opioid", "ome"),
               strata = "robotic",
               addOverall = TRUE,
               test=FALSE)

kableone(table_1)
```








