```







:::

@fig-sim-density Demonstrates the distribution of the dose (in mg) of each medication (@fig-sim-dose-density) and the distribution of OME values (in OME mg) by surgery type (@fig-sim-ome-density). @fig-sim-ome-proportion shoes that within the robotic and non-robotic groups the proportion of each opioid is not equal, and the effect of the oxycodone shortage has had a larger impact on the robotic group.








```{r}
obs_robotic_ome <- df %>% filter(robotic == "Robotic") %>% summarise(mean(ome)) %>% pull() 
obs_non_robotic_ome <- df %>% filter(robotic != "Robotic") %>% summarise(mean(ome)) %>% pull()
obs_robot_effect_mg <- obs_robotic_ome - obs_non_robotic_ome
```








The mean OME value for the robotic surgery group was `r round(obs_robotic_ome, 1)` OME mg and for the non-robotic surgery group was `r  round(obs_non_robotic_ome, 1)` OME mg. This gives an observed effect size of the robot as `r round(obs_robot_effect_mg,1)` OME mg. 








```{r}
result <- analyse_dataset(df)
```








Using a two sided t-test to assess if there is a significant difference in mean OME between the robotic and standard surgery groups, the mean difference was `r round(result$mean_diff_ome,1)` OME mg (95% CI `r paste(round(result$conf_int_ome, 1), collapse = " to ")`) with a p-value of `r signif(result$p_value_ome, 3)`.

Thus the researched from St Somewhere Hospital were able to conclude that the robotic surgery was associated with a significantly different post-operative OME dose. 

### Impact of conversion set and proportions

The study was then recreated using a grid search of across different conversion values and different proportion of post-operative oxycodone prescribing. The prior was informed by ranges observed in the literature and the latter to infulence the degree of different in opioid proportions between groups.








```{r}
#| label: fig-sim-grid-search-1

if (PRODUCTION_QUALITY) {
  trials = 100
} else {
  trials = 3
}

simulation1 <- run_simulation(trials=trials,
               conversion_values = seq(from=1, to=3, by = 0.2),
               morphine_proportion = seq(from =0.1, to= 0.9, by = 0.05)
               )
plot_grid(simulation1,
          potency_oxycodone_truth = potency_oxycodone_truth,
          robot_effect_mg = robot_effect_mg)

```








Given a `r mu_tot` mg expected opioid requirement and a treatment effect of `r scales::percent(robot_effect_size-1)` mg we would expect the mean difference in OME of the robotic group to be `r (mu_tot * robot_effect_size)-mu_tot` mg OME.

The results shown in @fig-sim-grid-search-1 show that where the proportions of morphine morphine prescribed post-op, (thus meaning a shortage of oxycodone has little influence on prescribing patterns and hence the effect of the exogenous shock is little) then across the values of $c_{\text{Oxycodone}}$, the true difference in treatment groups is well observed. With all situations being statistically significant. However as the proportion of post-operative prescribing shifts towards oxycodone, the observed difference in OME between the two groups becomes highly sensitive to the conversion value used. This is due to the exogenous effect of the oxycodone shortage impacting the robotic group more than the the standard surgery group. 

In this study set up, the true effect of the robot is powerful with an true effect of -10mg which is equal to 1 SD. Power calculations in @sec-appendix-simulation-power demonstrate that this is a very highly powered study. 








```{r}
robot_effect_mg = -4
robot_effect_size = (mu_tot + robot_effect_mg)/mu_tot

```








The experiment was run again but with a more modest effect of `r robot_effect_mg`mg or a post-operative OME `r scales::percent(robot_effect_size)` percent of the non-robotic group.








```{r}
#| label: fig-sim-grid-search-2

if (PRODUCTION_QUALITY) {
  trials = 100
} else {
  trials = 3
}

#Note that the robot_effect_size vairable is hard coded to the global_env above 
simulation2 <- run_simulation(trials=trials,
               conversion_values = seq(from=1, to=3.5, by = 0.2),
               morphine_proportion = seq(from =0.1, to= 0.9, by = 0.05)
               )
plot_grid_2 <- plot_grid(simulation2,
          potency_oxycodone_truth = potency_oxycodone_truth,
          robot_effect_mg = robot_effect_mg)

```








The parameters of this exemplar simulation can be mapped to the previous simple simulation. Firstly the values of $\delta p_i$ are $\epsilon$ are recovered for each trial in the grid search. These are then plotted side by side with the results of the first simulation when the same parameters as the exemplaer experiment is used. @fig-compare demonstrates that the experiment space for the example covered only values where $\Delta p_i < 0$ and for values of $ -0.5 < \epsilon < 1 $. Despite being a relativity narrow section of the possible gird space detailed in @sec-manuscript-results-simple with a modest effect size of `r robot_effect_mg` OME mg there are still values where the observed effect size is significantly different. 








```{r}
#| label: fig-compare
#| fig-title: Comparsion of simple simulation results and exemplar simulation results. 


label_function <- function(val) {
  paste0('mu["OME"]^"(control)" == ', val )
}

bounds <- c(min(simulation2$delta_p_oxycodone),
            max(simulation2$delta_p_oxycodone),
            min(simulation2$epsilon),
            max(simulation2$epsilon))

simple_results <- data.frame(p = numeric(), e = numeric(), diff = numeric())
for(p in seq(p_min, p_max, by = 0.05)){
  for(e in seq(e_min, e_max, by = 0.05)){
    
    diff <- sim_simple(
      n = n,
      delta_p_i = p,
      sigma = sigma,
      mu_opioid_requirment_control = mu_tot,
      potency_oxycodone = 2,
      error_in_c_oxycodone = e,
      treatment_effect = robot_effect_size -1
    )
    
    simple_results <- rbind(simple_results, data.frame(p = p, e = e, diff = diff))
  }
}


plot_simple_grid <- ggplot(simple_results, aes(y = p, x = e, fill = diff)) +
  geom_tile() +
  labs(,
       y = TeX(r'($\Delta  p_i$ (Difference in proportion))'),
       x = TeX(r'($\epsilon$ (Error in Conversion Value for Oxycodone) )'),
       fill = TeX(r'(Observed difference ($\Delta \mu_{{OME}})$)')
       ) +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(
    axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 45, hjust = 1),
    panel.grid = element_blank(),
    legend.position = "none")+
  symmetric_fill_scale(20)+
  geom_rect(ymin=bounds[1],
            ymax=bounds[2],
            xmin=bounds[3],
            xmax=bounds[4],
            colour="black",
            fill=alpha("grey",0))+
  annotate("text", label = "Parameter space of exemplar simulation", x=mean(bounds[3:4]), y=bounds[2]+0.1)

plot_grid_2 <- ggplot(simulation2, aes(x=conversion_value, y=morphine_proportion, fill=mean_diff_ome)) +
  geom_tile() +
  symmetric_fill_scale(20)+
  labs(y = TeX(r'(Proportion of post-op perscribing as morphine )'),
       x = TeX(r'($\c_{oxycodone}$ (Conversion Value for Oxycodone used) )')) +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(
    axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 45, hjust = 1),
    panel.grid = element_blank(),
    legend.position = "bottom")
  
layout <- c(
  area(t = 1, l = 1, b = 10, r = 10),
  area(t = 6, l = 11, b = 7, r = 20)
)

plot_simple_grid + plot_grid_2 +
  plot_layout(design = layout)

```








### Simulation of ...et al 

The final simulation looks at a study performed by xx et al. This study looked at the effect of xxx on yyyy and found a observed effect size of xxx. 

They did not report the difference of proportion of medications between their treatment groups. We will apply conversion values that have been used across the literature to calculate. One issue is that we do not know the true conversion values of the medications. 

# Conclusion

We have demonstrated that the use of opioid equivalence metrics such as OME or MME are sensitivity to the proportions of different opioids and the conversion sets used. We have demonstrated that the difference measured between the observed $\mu_{\text{OME}}^{(A)}$ and $\mu_{\text{OME}}^{(B)}$ is a product of not only the association of the two groups (be it statistical or causal), but also the difference in proportions of medications within each group. This is explicitly demonstrated in @sec-ome-difference-proof. 

In addition, the opioid equivalence metric is also sensitivity to any error in the true conversion value of the underlying population and the conversion value used for calculations. As expressed across the literature, it is difficult to ascertain all of the inter-patient difference and inter-opioid interactions that would provide the true conversion value. None the less, any difference in the true population level conversion value and the values used will also lead to systematic under or over estimation of the true value. 

Where the opioid equivalence metric is being used as outcome metric in a comparison between groups, any difference in proportion of differing opioids or the conversion values will lead to a systematic under or over estimation of the true different. If either one of these is exactly correct then the error will cancel out but if both are not matched it can lead to an up to 3 fold error in the estimate of difference. 

This work is particular relevant to retrospective observational work where large datasets with varied treatment lend themselves well to the tool of opioid conversion values. However, even in a RCT where the protocol for analgesia may be fixed and patients randomised between groups, confounding may occur if analgesia protocols contain differing opioids allowing the possibility of diverging proportions between treatment groups. 


## Implications



## Limitations

In the simulation studies we have only considered situations where medication choice is binary between the reference medication and the alternative (oxycodone). We hypothesise that in some situations with multiple opioids, due to Orthogonality, that multiple small errors in the estimate of $c_i$ and the true value of $c_i$ may generally cancel out and thus have little impact on the final estimate. This may be achieved using machine learning tools that consider the co-linearity between the features of the dose of each medication and the outcome of total opioid burden or OME. 

## Recommendations:

1. All studies using opioid equivalence metrics must publish the proportions of the differing opioids and conversion value sets used for each group. 

2. More work is required to estimate the true population level opioid conversion value sets to reduce the error between the true unmeasurable potency and the value sets used. 

